{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And Isaac's servants dug in the valley, and found there a well of living water.</b> The Ramban wrote, and likewise in Sefer Toldot Yitzchak and likewise in Sefer Menorat HaMa'or: since everything that happened to the fathers was a sign for the children, therefore they found a place to expound all these matters of the wells upon the three Temples, which were called a well of living water. For just as they made strife over two wells, and the third he called Rehovot, so too in the First and Second Temples the nations made strife with Israel until they destroyed them, but the third, which will be built speedily in our days, he called Rehovot." "And I shall add of my own, to broaden the details of the matters so that they accord with this exposition, and in it will be answered what is stated: 'for now HaShem has made room for us, and we shall be fruitful in the land.' For as it appears, the statement 'and we shall be fruitful in the land' is not a reason for the expression Rehovot; therefore I shall broaden the discourse in it through the details of the matters, all of which correspond to what has happened to us and what will be in the end of days. For it is well known what our Sages of blessed memory said (Yoma 9b) that in the First Temple there was factionalism and strife and baseless hatred among the princes of Israel, who were called shepherds, as it is written (Jeremiah 23:4), ״וַהֲקִימֹתִי עֲלֵיהֶם רֹעִים״. And the Temple was destroyed because of the strife that was between the kings of Israel and the kings of Judah. And in the Second Temple the quality of baseless hatred was among all Israel, for the plague spread among them beyond any cure. And therefore it is written concerning the first well, ״וַיָּרִיבוּ רֹעֵי גְרָר עִם רֹעֵי יִצְחָק״, to hint also to the future, that there would be strife between the shepherds of Israel, and along with it the shepherds of the nations also multiplied against Israel. Therefore he called the name of the well Esek, for they contended with him — for there is a difference between two who quarrel over some matter of business between them, and two who quarrel for nothing about nothing, as is the custom of our generation. And this is the difference between the First Temple and the Second: for the hatred that was among the shepherds of Israel in the First Temple was over the matter of the kingdom, for the kingdom of Israel was divided, and the nature of the matter dictates that one kingdom does not touch its fellow; therefore each one thought that his fellow was encroaching on his territory, and through this dispute the enmity grew among them." "But the baseless hatred of the Second Temple was for nothing, about nothing, for even the lowliest hated one another for no reason at all. Therefore their hatred is called baseless hatred, even though one had no business whatsoever with his fellow — this is nothing but evil of the heart. And the Satan was dancing among them to cause them to stumble, one through the other, for HaShem found their iniquity from another place; therefore he incited them one against the other in order to find cause for hatred. Therefore, ״וַיַּחְפְּרוּ בְאֵר אַחֶרֶת וַיָּרִיבוּ גַּם עָלֶיהָ וַיִּקְרָא שְׁמָהּ שִׂטְנָה״ (Genesis 26:21) — he did not mention the shepherds in this strife, alluding to the strife of the Second Temple, which was among all Israel. Therefore they called it Sitnah, for they found no cause for their quarrels other than what was surely the Satan dancing between their horns, with which they would gore one another, as it is written (Leviticus 26:37), ״וְכָשְׁלוּ אִישׁ בְּאָחִיו״." "And over the third well they did not quarrel, for the Third Temple will be built through King Messiah, of whom it is stated (Isaiah 9:6), ״לְמַרְבֵּה הַמִּשְׂרָה וּלְשָׁלוֹם אֵין קֵץ״ — for only peace and truth will be in his days. Therefore it is called ״רְחוֹבוֹת״, for then HaShem will broaden their borders. For at a time when strife is prevalent, the two sides are hostile; even when they are in a great city like Antiochia, it could not bear them to dwell together, and even in a place very broad of hands the place is too cramped for them and cannot contain them — as, in our sins, this custom still persists among us today. And in the opposite manner: at a time when peace is upon Israel, even though we are fruitful in the land and the inhabitants upon it become very numerous, nevertheless the place is broad of hands for them and nothing is cramped — as is concluded in tractate Sanhedrin, chapter 1 (7a): 'When there was affection, [even] the narrow rim of a seah-measure would hold them; now that there is not much affection, a threshing-floor of sixty kor does not suffice for us,' end quote. Thus it is stated, ״כִּי הִרְחִיב ה׳ לָנוּ״ — even though we are fruitful in the land and the inhabitants upon it are many, nevertheless the land before them is broad of hands. And furthermore, that we find in the Second Temple that because of the discord, whole groups and also many [others] left the land because of the wickedness of those who dwelt in it; therefore he said that at the time of the establishment of peace, 'we shall be fruitful in the land' — for they will not need to leave it."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:30:35.463801819Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}